\title{Completeness of Layered Dynamic Programming over Ordered Weight Monoids}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop from first principles a general theory of \emph{layered dynamic programming} --- the
Bellman/Viterbi schema --- over an arbitrary finite non-empty state space $S$ and an arbitrary
linearly ordered commutative additive monoid $W$ of weights whose addition is monotone. Within
this setting we prove a \textbf{completeness theorem}: every labelling $f : \mathbb{N} \to S$ is
dominated by some \emph{run} of the dynamic program, i.e.\ there exists a labelling $g$ all of
whose prefixes are optimal and whose total score at every fixed horizon is at least that of $f$.
Together with the dual \textbf{soundness} statement --- every run is an honest labelling whose
score is exactly the value computed by the recursion --- this yields an \textbf{exactness
theorem}: the forward value function $V(n,s)$ is the \emph{greatest element} of the set of scores
achievable by labellings ending in state $s$ at stage $n$. Around this core we establish Bellman's
optimality principle, an exact characterisation of runs as the labellings optimal among those with
the same endpoint, a forward--backward decomposition, a tropical walk calculus with a max-plus
Chapman--Kolmogorov identity, monotonicity, equivariance and Lipschitz stability of the optimum
under perturbation, order duality yielding the min-plus (shortest-path) theory for free, and a
removal of the cancellativity hypothesis via a structural \emph{backtrace} notion of a run. The
last item makes the theory applicable to \emph{constrained} dynamic programming over
$W \cup \{\bot\}$, where $\bot$ is an absorbing ``infeasible'' weight and cancellativity fails;
there we obtain a clean characterisation of infeasibility and instantiate the theory on
maximum-weight independent set on a path.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{dynamic programming, Bellman optimality principle, Viterbi algorithm, completeness,
tropical semiring, max-plus algebra, ordered monoid, constrained optimisation}

